WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.967
Month one was a loop: read, change
one definition, sign, read again.

00:00:04.967 --> 00:00:07.400
Now change what GTM OS itself looks like.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.867
Generate a screen from your data,
package your method for clients,

00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:14.333
and take updates that never
overwrite what your team changed.

00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.633
In this clip you'll generate a screen
and know the three kinds of package.

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:20.400
You'll also take an update
through a three way merge.

00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:23.533
Leo's agency builds the same
dashboards for every client.

00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:27.600
Then a release lands, and the
copy one client edited breaks.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.233
Fork, and you fall behind. Overwrite,
and you lose their changes.

00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:32.933
GTM OS does neither.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.033
First, every screen in the console,

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:38.500
from Mission Control to the
engine pages, is an app.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.400
It's a definition, with no custom code.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.433
The framework has view types: record
page, queue, table, dashboard, form,

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.867
wizard, approval card, list and spotlight.

00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:51.933
Every button is an operation from
the same registry your agent uses,

00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:54.200
so a screen can never do
what an agent couldn't.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.333
And brand tokens restyle all of it at
once. In the console: Apps, All apps.

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:01.133
Second, generate one.

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.333
gtm app generate scaffolds a screen from

00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:05.900
an object type and the
live operation registry.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.533
Pass a kind: record page,
queue, table or dashboard.

00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:11.767
You get an app definition
to review and edit.

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:15.900
It goes in as a ChangeSet like any
other, so a different person approves it.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.133
Or ask your agent: "Create a queue
screen for accounts that need review".

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.433
Third, packages.

00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:25.467
Everything installable is a package:
engines, screens, connectors,

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:28.467
modules and blueprints.
There are three tiers.

00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.867
Declarative packages carry
definitions and no code,

00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:35.033
and they're the default choice.
Trusted packages run code in process,

00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.700
and sandboxed packages run it in
isolation. Both must be signed.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.333
An unsigned trusted or sandboxed
package is refused at install.

00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:45.300
In the console: Settings, Packages.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.967
Or ask your agent: "What
packages are installed".

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.067
Now the trust check.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.300
A package is signed by the first party key

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.800
or by a publisher key
you've listed as trusted.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.900
It declares a permission envelope,
the operations it may call,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.567
and its writes stay inside it.

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:01.933
An egress guard controls what
it can reach outside GTM OS.

00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:04.433
Fourth, agencies package their method.

00:02:04.433 --> 00:02:08.233
A declarative package of definitions,
with parameters per client,

00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:10.133
installs with one
proposal and one approval.

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:12.533
And gtm pkg provenance shows which agency

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:15.300
package and which version
an organization runs.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.867
Because it's only definitions,

00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:19.200
the client can read all
of it before approving.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.067
Or ask your agent: "Which agency
package and version do we run".

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:25.667
Last, updates arrive without taking over.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:28.167
In the console: Settings, Packages.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:31.433
A card says engine apps have
updates and shows what each adds,

00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:32.567
changes or removes per environment.

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.700
Only an owner or admin
can propose the update,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.333
and a second person approves it.

00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:41.633
It's a three way merge: the version
you started from, the new one,

00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:42.367
and your edited copy.

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:45.133
Where they overlap you see
both, and you choose Keep mine.

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:47.800
Edited copies are never overwritten,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.700
and every update is a new config
version you can roll back.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.200
Or ask your agent: "Do any of
our engine apps have updates".

00:02:54.233 --> 00:02:58.200
Here's the doubt: someone else's code
in my stack? Mostly it isn't code.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.200
A declarative package is only
definitions you can read.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.500
Trusted and sandboxed code must be signed,

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.833
can call only the
operations in its envelope,

00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:07.700
and sits behind an egress guard.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.533
And an update to an installed
app stays a human decision,

00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:12.767
because agent keys are refused for it.

00:03:12.833 --> 00:03:14.800
Shipped apps get better with each release,

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.900
and you take them on your schedule.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:20.533
Edit an app in the console or by
ChangeSet. Restyle it with brand tokens,

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.933
or package your changes so another
organization can install them.

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:25.733
Studio can also draft an app from a

00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:28.233
sentence and ship it
into dev behind approval.

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:30.933
Advanced move: prefer a
declarative package over code.

00:03:30.933 --> 00:03:34.633
It's the easiest for a client to
review and the hardest to get wrong.

00:03:34.633 --> 00:03:38.467
Reach for trusted or sandboxed code
only when definitions can't do the job.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.233
Approvals gate every change here.

00:03:40.233 --> 00:03:42.667
Environments move apps
from dev to production.

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.100
And Upgrading is where
new defaults arrive.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:49.333
Quick pass: Screens are definitions,
packages are signed and reviewable,

00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:53.933
and updates merge instead of overwriting.
Add by definition, never by fork.

00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:57.767
Now you can extend GTM OS with
screens and packages that are signed,

00:03:57.767 --> 00:03:59.800
readable and safe to update.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.200
Next: Self hosting on a server, your
own instance, on your own domain,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.367
closed to everything except a proxy.
